# Line endings: default everything to LF, and list the exceptions.
#
# v1.26.96 — this used to be a whitelist, one rule per file or extension. That shape
# guarantees a repeat: 906 tracked files, 28 covered, and the file that broke
# (hooks/ownmind-git-commit-msg) was simply added after the list was written. A list does
# not report what it is missing. Reported as bugs #17 and #19.
#
# `text=auto` is load-bearing and must not be dropped. Writing `* eol=lf` on its own sets
# `text` unconditionally, which switches OFF git's binary sniffing — measured on a file
# containing 0x0D 0x0A: 13 bytes in, 11 bytes out, corrupted. With `text=auto`, git detects
# binary content and leaves it alone. This repository has two such files today, one of them
# CHANGELOG.md, which carries four literal NUL bytes from an old entry about handling NUL
# bytes.
#
# Why it matters at all: a CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name
# ends in a carriage return. Git for Windows tolerates it; WSL's sh and busybox do not.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native formats keep CRLF. These come after the global rule on purpose — last
# match wins.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.vbs  text eol=crlf

# Deliberate NUL bytes, and the file's whole purpose is to pin exact content. `text=auto`
# would already skip it, so this is belt and braces — but it also documents the intent, and
# it survives someone narrowing the rule above.
# `-eol` as well as `-text`: setting `eol` alone enables conversion and effectively sets
# `text`, so unsetting only `text` would leave it converted.
tests/install-check-null-byte-sanitize.test.js  -text -eol
